<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/colonialism-and-empire">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Colonialism is the history of <strong>one part of the world organising another for its own
    benefit</strong> — for trade, settlement, prestige and resource extraction — from ~1500 to
    the 1960s. It reshaped demography (the largest forced migration ever), ecology (the Columbian
    exchange), economies (global trade built on coercion) and politics (borders and institutions
    drawn by outsiders). No honest world history skips it; no serious treatment flattens it into
    one story.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four structural facts of the colonial era" widget="equationStep" config={colCfg} />

  <h2>Reading it critically</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>"Civilising" claims vs the record:</strong> colonial administrations built some railways and schools — primarily to extract; literacy remained deliberately limited in most empires (Belgian Congo's university graduates on independence: under 20).</li>
    <li><strong>Resistance was constant:</strong> from Túpac Amaru to the Ashanti wars to Gandhi — empires spent continuously on suppression, and the costs (and World War exhaustion) mattered to the endgame.</li>
    <li><strong>Historiographical shift:</strong> older "empire brought civilisation" narratives gave way to subaltern studies (reading colonial archives against their grain) and to centreing Indigenous and enslaved voices — the discipline applying its own method to its own blind spots.</li>
    <li><strong>The ledger is still open:</strong> reparations debates, museum repatriation, and persistent trade asymmetries are colonial legacies in current politics — history as a live document.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hochschild, King Leopold's Ghost; Crosby, The Columbian Exchange;Slave Voyages database (slavevoyages.org); Said, Orientalism (1978).</p>
</TopicPage>
